{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For nuclear equations: check both mass number and atomic number balance",
   "Alpha: mass −4, atomic number −2. Beta: mass same, atomic number +1",
   "For half-life: keep halving the initial value until you reach the target",
   "Count the halvings, then use: half-life = total time ÷ number of halvings"
  ],
  "title": "Nuclear Equations and Half-Life",
  "content": "<p>Two different skills that look very different but both come down to <strong>counting</strong>.</p><p><strong>Nuclear equations:</strong> Both the top numbers (mass numbers) and the bottom numbers (atomic numbers) must balance on each side. This lets you fill in any missing value.</p><ul><li><strong>Alpha decay:</strong> emits a helium nucleus — mass number drops by 4, atomic number drops by 2</li><li><strong>Beta decay:</strong> a neutron turns into a proton — mass number stays the same, atomic number goes up by 1</li></ul><p><strong>Half-life:</strong> The time for half the radioactive nuclei to decay. There is no equation to learn — just keep halving.</p><ul><li>Count how many times you halve to reach the target value</li><li>Each halving = one half-life</li><li>To find the half-life: total time ÷ number of halvings</li><li>After \\(n\\) half-lives, the fraction remaining = \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\)</li></ul>"
 },
